  "name": "FACTOR - VII FUNCTIONAL",
  "code": "1540",
  "cost": 0,
  "interpretation": "<p><span style=\"font-size: 11px;\">Factor VII deficiency is relatively common as compared with other rare hereditary coagulation factor deficiencies. Mild to moderate deficiency is associated with epistaxis, mucosal bleeding &amp; menorrhagia. Severe deficiency is autosomal recessive, presenting shortly after birth with&nbsp; ntracranial hemorrhage. Factor VII is a vitamin K dependent clotting factor which is low in the newborns and in the presence of vitamin K deficiency. It is associated with normal PTT but prolonged PT.</span><br><br><span style=\"font-size: 11px;\"><strong>Decreased levels</strong></span></p>\n<ul>\n<li style=\"font-size: 11px;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11px;\">Inherited deficiency</span></li>\n<li style=\"font-size: 11px;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11px;\">Acquired due to liver disease, vitamin K deficiency or anticoagulant therapy</span></li>\n</ul>\n<p><span style=\"font-size: 11px;\"><strong>Increased levels</strong></span><br><span style=\"font-size: 11px;\">Pregnancy, Oral contraceptives, hyperlipidemia, ageing &amp; obesity</span></p>",
